# Target C Section 116 companion — exact exceptional-face-count form

The zero-host theorem has an exact simplification that is obscured by the coarse
`P_exc<=5Q` corollary.

Let `f_exc` be the number of bounded nonquadrilateral faces of the harmonic carrier.
By definition of the bounded-face degree excess,

\[
Q=\sum_{f\ \rm bounded}(\deg f-4),
\]

and every bounded quadrilateral is a retained strict rhombus.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
P_{\rm exc}
:=\sum_{f\ \rm nonquad}\deg f
=4f_{\rm exc}+Q.
}
\tag{116.F1}
\]

Canonical Section 82 gives the exact face ledger

\[
\boxed{L+Q=4a-6-2s-2c.}
\tag{116.F2}
\]

Subtracting `Q` in (116.F1)--(116.F2) gives the cancellation

\[
\boxed{
L+P_{\rm exc}
=4a-6-2s-2c+4f_{\rm exc}.
}
\tag{116.F3}
\]

Hence the exact two-arc zero-host return theorem is equivalently

\[
\boxed{
 d\bigl(
 4a-6-2s-2c+4f_{\rm exc}-2d
 \bigr)
 \ge(4-2\sqrt3)A_4.
}
\tag{116.F4}
\]

The three-arc theorem likewise becomes

\[
\boxed{
 d\bigl(
 4a-6-2s-2c+4f_{\rm exc}-\Pi_3^{\max}
 \bigr)
 \ge(2-\sqrt3)A_4.
}
\tag{116.F5}
\]

And the one-closed-outer-walk form is

\[
\boxed{
 d\bigl(4a-6-2s-2c+4f_{\rm exc}\bigr)
 \ge4A_4.
}
\tag{116.F6}
\]

A useful rearrangement of (116.F4) is the explicit exceptional-face lower bound

\[
\boxed{
 f_{\rm exc}
 \ge
 {1\over4}
 \left[
 { (4-2\sqrt3)A_4\over d}
 -4a+2d+6+2s+2c
 \right].
}
\tag{116.F7}
\]

The right side may be negative, in which case the statement is vacuous; when it is
positive, however, it forces a linear exceptional-face population directly from
signed return.  Since `f_exc<=Q`, the same positive lower bound also applies to `Q`.

The important conceptual point is that the zero-host treatment pays each bounded
defect face once as a closed return cycle.  Its degree does not survive in the final
exact ledger: all degree-excess currency cancels against the outer-face identity.

This corollary is finite, source-level, and independent of the near-endpoint wrapping
analysis.  It does not by itself close C-II.
